Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC)
Respiratory System
Persistent cough/hemoptysis, Unexplained weight loss, Spiculated mass on CT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-029
Reason for Visit: New-onset localized wheezing, worsening deep aching chest pain, and repeated episodes of coughing up blood requiring urgent clinical evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with a heavy smoking history who has been coughing up blood and experiencing worsening chest pain and weight loss. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate the severity of the hemoptysis and localized airway obstruction, and to initiate urgent specialist diagnostic pathways before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have lost 10 kilos without trying, my chest constantly aches deep inside, and every morning I cough up bright red blood streaks.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing over the last 3 months. The patient notes the new, progressive, and changing character of a persistent cough that they used to consider just a "smoker's cough." They report intermittent hemoptysis of varying severity over the past 2 weeks. There is significant unexplained weight loss and cachexia. The patient complains of progressive exertional dyspnea and a dull, deep aching chest pain on the left side that is not relieved by simple analgesia.
Functional Status: Independent but visibly fatigued and slowing down.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding the hemoptysis; 40 pack-year smoking history.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 breaths/min | SpO2: 95% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg (Down 10Kg in 3 months)
General: Cachectic, frail appearance. Appears anxious.
Physical Exam: Chest examination reveals dullness to percussion over the left upper lobe. Auscultation demonstrates focal wheezing strictly localized to the left upper zone, indicating localized airway obstruction. Digital clubbing is clearly present on bilateral hands. Palpation of the neck reveals a firm supraclavicular lymphadenopathy on the left side. The clinical tetrad of persistent cough/hemoptysis, unexplained weight loss, and localized signs strongly suggests an underlying malignancy.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of recent smoking in the home.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter cough suppressants with no effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC) - Status: Suspected
Differentials:

Pulmonary Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever or drenching night sweats, and presence of focal wheeze and fixed supraclavicular lymphadenopathy strongly pointing to a solid tumor.

Chronic Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Localized focal wheeze rather than widespread rhonchi, massive weight loss, and new hemoptysis.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic 3-month progressive history of weight loss, clubbing, and deep aching pain rather than sudden acute pleuritic pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an alarming array of "red flag" symptoms: changing persistent cough, hemoptysis, severe unexplained weight loss, and deep chest pain. The physical findings of a fixed supraclavicular lymph node, digital clubbing, and a fixed focal wheeze point overwhelmingly to a space-occupying malignant lesion (NSCLC) causing localized airway obstruction. While the condition is chronic, the escalating hemoptysis and severe pain required an urgent after-hours assessment to rule out massive hemorrhage, provide adequate immediate analgesia for the somatic pain, and bypass routine wait times by mandating an immediate, urgent diagnostic pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed regular Paracetamol and an NSAID for the deep aching chest pain. Generated a marked "URGENT - Suspected Malignancy" referral for a contrast CT chest and respiratory physician review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Write down exactly how much blood you cough up each day and what it looks like (e.g., streaks, clots, spoonfuls) in a dedicated notebook; recording this specific data provides your specialist with an accurate assessment of the bleeding risk, directly guiding whether you need an emergency procedure.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Patient must present to the radiology clinic first thing in the morning for the urgent CT scan (looking for a spiculated mass); GP review within 48 hours for results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) to the Emergency Department if the patient coughs up more than a tablespoon of fresh, bright red blood in a single episode, or if they experience sudden, severe shortness of breath.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: These clinical signs are highly suspicious for a serious lung condition, including cancer, but only a CT scan and tissue biopsy can provide a definitive diagnosis.
Clinical Course: The pain medication will take the edge off the chest ache, but definitive treatment will depend entirely on the results of the upcoming scans.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest emergency department immediately if you begin coughing up large amounts of fresh blood or blood clots.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The focal wheeze means a localized lump is pressing on one of your airways, and the weight loss suggests your body is fighting something significant; the urgent CT scan is required immediately to identify exactly what this lump is.
Actionable Steps: You must completely stop taking any aspirin or blood-thinning supplements unless prescribed by a doctor; these medications thin the blood and will directly increase the risk of a minor bleed in your lung turning into a life-threatening hemorrhage.

